description:
`runPerformanceRegression` could not detect a performance regression. It reported success unconditionally.

```ts
const hasRegression = false          // never reassigned

await runFullPerformanceSuite(ciOptions)

// TODO: Load baseline and compare results
if (baselineFile) {
  consola.info(`Would compare against baseline: ${baselineFile}`)
  // Implement baseline comparison logic here
}

if (hasRegression) {
  consola.error('Performance regression detected!')
  process.exit(1)
} else {
  consola.success('No performance regression detected')
}
```

The baseline comparison was never written. `hasRegression` is a `const false`, so the `process.exit(1)` branch is unreachable. Passing a baseline file logs `Would compare against ...` and changes nothing — the run still ends in `No performance regression detected`.

It was reachable through `runBenchmarkCLI`'s `case 'regression'`, so wiring `tsx src/test/performance-runner.ts regression <baseline>` into CI would have produced a permanently green gate that had never checked anything. That is worse than having no check, because it looks like coverage.

## Why delete rather than fix

Nothing depends on it. No `package.json` script exposes the `regression` mode, no workflow invokes it, and no documentation promises it — every "regression" reference in the repo turned out to be the Playwright/Storybook visual regression suite, which is unrelated.

A real performance gate needs decisions this stub never made: where baselines are stored, how they are recorded and refreshed, what threshold counts as a regression, and how noisy CI runners are handled. None of that is recoverable from `// Implement baseline comparison logic here`. If the capability is wanted, it should be designed against those questions rather than grown from a stub that has been reporting success since it was written. [description truncated to fit budget]
